def rerank_chunks(query, chunks):
    query_keywords = ["POS tagging", "part-of-speech tagging", "POS tag", "part-of-speech tag", "POS-tagging", "part-of-speech-tagging",
                      "shallow parsing", "NP chunking", "noun phrase chunking", "chunking",
                      "semi-supervised learning", "semi-supervised", "semi supervised learning", "semi supervised",
                      "unlabeled data", "unlabelled data", "unlabeled corpus", "unlabelled corpus", "unlabeled", "unlabelled",
                      "NER", "named entity recognition", "named entity recognizer", "named entity tagging", "NER system",
                      "CRF", "conditional random fields", "CRFs"]

    chunk_scores = []
    for index, chunk in enumerate(chunks):
        score = 0
        chunk_lower = chunk.lower()
        for keyword in query_keywords:
            keyword_lower = keyword.lower()
            if keyword_lower in chunk_lower:
                if keyword_lower in ["pos tagging", "part-of-speech tagging", "pos tag", "part-of-speech tag", "pos-tagging", "part-of-speech-tagging"]:
                    score += 5 * chunk_lower.count(keyword_lower)
                elif keyword_lower in ["shallow parsing", "np chunking", "noun phrase chunking", "chunking"]:
                    score += 4 * chunk_lower.count(keyword_lower)
                elif keyword_lower in ["semi-supervised learning", "semi-supervised", "semi supervised learning", "semi supervised"]:
                    score += 4 * chunk_lower.count(keyword_lower)
                elif keyword_lower in ["unlabeled data", "unlabelled data", "unlabeled corpus", "unlabelled corpus", "unlabeled", "unlabelled"]:
                    score += 3 * chunk_lower.count(keyword_lower)
                elif keyword_lower in ["ner", "named entity recognition", "named entity recognizer", "named entity tagging", "ner system"]:
                    score += 3 * chunk_lower.count(keyword_lower)
                elif keyword_lower in ["crf", "conditional random fields", "crfs"]:
                    score += 2 * chunk_lower.count(keyword_lower)
        chunk_scores.append((score, index))

    ranked_chunks_indices = [index for score, index in sorted(chunk_scores, key=lambda item: item[0], reverse=True)]
    return ranked_chunks_indices

chunks = ['used in our experiment is the version available from the NLTK toolkit, with BFGS optimizer. For CRF, we use the linear-chain CRF model available from the Mallet package 1 . Bootstrapping for Dictionary Expansion The supervised learning approach assumes the existence of an annotated set of training data. Often times, training data must be painstakingly marked up and collecting large-scale labeled training examples can be very costly. In recent years, more and more research effort has been focused on how to leverage a vast amount of unlabeled data in a semisupervised or entirely unsupervised fashion for NER as well as for other similar NLP tasks, e.g. POS tagging, sentence boundary detection, and word sense disambiguation (Riloff 1999; Ghani and Jones 2002; Probst et al. 2006; Brody and Elhadad 2010; Haghighi 2010) . One way to incorporate a vast amount of unlabeled data is to learn a clustering of words that assigns syntactically similar words to the same clusters. Popular clustering algorithms used', 'literal word tests, patterns of orthography, parts of speech, semantic categories, or membership in special-purpose gazetteers. While supervised training greatly facilitates the development of a robust NER system, the requirement of a substantial training corpus remains an impediment to the rapid deployment of NER in new domains or new languages. A number bush peters reagan noriega ... john robert james david ... president chairman head owner ... japan california london chicago ... of researchers have therefore sought to exploit the availability of unlabeled documents, typically by bootstrapping a classifier using automatic labellings (Collins and Singer, 1999; Cucerzan and Yarowsky, 1999; Thelen and Riloff, 2002) . Here, we investigate a different approach. Using a distributional clustering technique called coclustering, we produce clusters which, intuitively, should be useful for NER. Table 1 shows example terms from several sample clusters induced using a